<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polybius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 200–c. 118 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Polybius was a Greek historian from Megalopolis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a>, taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as a hostage in 167 BCE, where he became a friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus">Scipio Aemilianus</a> and witnessed the destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> in 146 BCE. His Histories, in 40 books, explain how Rome came to dominate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> between 264 and 146 BCE. He is valued for his use of sources and his theory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixed-constitution">mixed constitution</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polybius.jpg" alt="Polybius" width="255" height="512" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jona Lendering, Livius Onderwijs · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stele_des_Polybios.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Polybius</b> stresses that the members used the same laws, weights, measures and coinage, and citizens of one city probably enjoyed rights such as owning property and marrying in the others.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The fall of Macedon brought Greek culture to Rome on a new scale: Aemilius Paullus let his sons take Perseus' library, and among the thousand Achaean hostages deported to Italy was the historian <b>Polybius</b>, whose account of Rome's rise centres on the Macedonian wars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Polybius</b> described a society weakened by debt and public feasting.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Polybius</b>, in a famous description of the city, explains how the currents of the Bosporus brought ships to its harbours rather than to Chalcedon opposite, and how the city stood astride the Black Sea trade, which supplied the Greek world with slaves, cattle, honey, wax and salted fish…</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Polybius</b> and later writers praised the quality of their swords, and the Roman gladius hispaniensis is generally thought to derive from Iberian, perhaps Celtiberian, models.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>According to <b>Polybius</b>, by the late third century they had brought most of the island under their control, with the exception of Lyttos.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Megalopolis, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.41,22.13&amp;year=-147" aria-label="Megalopolis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5103 817 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Polybius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5553 1087h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5553 1087h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.41,22.13&amp;year=-147">What was here in 147 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the most instructive, or rather the only, method of learning to bear with dignity the vicissitudes of fortune is to recall the catastrophes of others.&#8221;</p><footer>Histories, book 1 &#183; tr. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh, 1889 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44125/pg44125-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…to keep well in mind in the midst of prosperity the mutability of Fortune, is the characteristic of a great man…&#8221;</p><footer>Histories, book 38 &#183; tr. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh, 1889 &#183; of Scipio Aemilianus at the fall of Carthage &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44126/pg44126-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
